Vite's `server.proxy` uses `http-proxy`, so you can set `auth` for the upstream and control header forwarding with `configure`. By default the client's `Authorization` header is forwarded, but setting `auth` makes http-proxy overwrite it with the upstream credentials—so you need to re-inject the original header yourself.

```js
export default {
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/api': {
        target: 'https://upstream.example.com',
        changeOrigin: true,
        auth: 'user:pass', // upstream basic auth
        configure: (proxy) => {
          proxy.on('proxyReq', (proxyReq, req) => {
            // restore the client's Authorization header
            if (req.headers.authorization) {
              proxyReq.setHeader('authorization', req.headers.authorization)
            }
          })
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Note: if the client's header is also Basic auth, the upstream will see the client's credentials instead of `user:pass`, so this only makes sense when the two auth schemes differ (e.g., client sends Bearer). I'm not certain of every http-proxy version's exact overwrite behavior, so verify with a test request.

