Vite treats `.svg` as a static asset by default, so a virtual module ID ending in `.svg` gets routed to the asset plugin (which tries to resolve it as a file on disk) rather than your virtual-module plugin. The asset plugin's `load`/`resolveId` hooks run before or conflict with yours, so the virtual ID never reaches your loader and you get a "failed to resolve" or "file not found" error.

Fix: give the virtual ID a non-asset extension (e.g. `virtual:icon.svg?raw` won't help—use `virtual:icon` or `virtual:icon.js`), or explicitly handle it in `resolveId` with `enforce: 'pre'` and return the ID so Vite doesn't fall through to the asset pipeline.

```js
{
  name: 'my-virtual-svg',
  enforce: 'pre',
  resolveId(id) {
    if (id.startsWith('virtual:icon')) return '\0' + id
  },
  load(id) {
    if (id.startsWith('\0virtual:icon')) return `export default "<svg>...</svg>"`
  }
}
```

The `\0` prefix also prevents other plugins from re-processing the ID. If you need the raw SVG string, prefer a `.js`-suffixed virtual ID and export the markup yourself.

